INT. ROLLER DERBY ARENA - HOME LOCKER ROOM - NIGHT

Cinderblock walls painted red. A single long bench. A cracked mirror reflects six women in various states of armor. The crowd's stomp thunders through the ceiling.

COACH ROSALIND "ROZ" ACHEBE-LUND, 50s, former jammer, a scar across one eyebrow, stands by the door, arms folded. She wears a faded jersey, number 00.

On the bench, from left to right: GWYNETH "GUTS" PARRISH-OKONKWO, 30s, pivot, taping her wrists; INDIRA "NAILS" CHAKRABORTY, 20s, blocker, brushing glitter across her cheekbones; MAUREEN "MO" DELACROIX-KELLY, 40s, the oldest skater, pumping her fist against her palm; and TALLULAH "TULLY" SEKIBO, 19, the rookie, ghost-white, holding her helmet like a hymnal. At the end sits BLANCA "BONES" ZARATE-WILLIAMS, 30s, jammer, headphones on, eyes closed.

ROZ
Listen up.

They do. Even Bones lowers a headphone.

ROZ (CONT'D)
We're down forty points. Second half. They have the best blockers in the league. We have a rookie and a prayer.

TULLY
(small)
Coach.

ROZ
Yes, Tully.

TULLY
I think I'm going to be sick.

ROZ
Good. Be sick now. Not on the track.

Tully nods. Swallows.

ROZ (CONT'D)
Guts, you're pivot. You set the pace. Nails, you take the inside lane. Mo, you cover Bones. Bones, you jam. Tully, you...

TULLY
Stand there?

ROZ
You block. And you stay on your skates.

GUTS
Coach, what's the plan?

ROZ
The plan is we don't lose.

NAILS
That's not a plan.

ROZ
It's the only one I've got.

MO
(laughing)
Roz, she's right.

BONES
(removing headphones)
It's a good plan.

Everyone looks at Bones. She rarely speaks.

BONES (CONT'D)
My plan is to go through them.

ROZ
Then go through them.

GUTS
And we follow.

NAILS
And we hit.

MO
And we hit again.

TULLY
And I stay on my skates.

ROZ
That's all I ask.

A buzzer sounds in the hall. Roz opens the door. Roar of the crowd floods in.

ROZ (CONT'D)
Ladies. Make me proud.

One by one they rise. Bones last. At the door she pauses, puts a hand on Tully's shoulder.

BONES
First hit is the hardest. After that it's just skating.

TULLY
Okay.

BONES
Okay.

They file out. Roz watches them go, jaw working. Then she follows them into the light.
